 NERVIOSA,\"Anorexia Nervosa (AN) is a serious eating disorder characterized by an intense fear of gaining weight and a distorted body image. This condition leads to self-imposed starvation, excessive weight loss, and restricted energy intake, resulting in significantly low body weight. Individuals with anorexia nervosa often engage in behaviors that interfere with weight gain, even when they are severely underweight. The disorder can cause severe health complications, including malnutrition, osteoporosis, hormonal imbalances, and primary amenorrhea in prepubertal patients. \n\nAnorexia nervosa raises complex medical and ethical issues, particularly regarding treatment and patient autonomy, especially in detention settings. It can lead to severe health risks, necessitating nutritional rehabilitation and, in some cases, enteral nutrition support. The disorder is also associated with physiological conditions such as hypercortisolism, although it rarely presents with clinical features of Cushing's syndrome. Overall, anorexia nervosa is a multifaceted mental health disorder that poses significant challenges for both patients and healthcare providers.\",49\r\n4027,FLUOXETINA,\"Fluoxetina, also known as fluoxetine, is a medication primarily used as an antidepressant. It is prescribed as part of treatment plans for various conditions, including depression and eating disorders. Fluoxetina has been studied for its effectiveness in reducing binge eating and purging behaviors in patients with eating disorders. Additionally, it is noted that fluoxetine can increase appetite in some individuals. However, it is important to be aware that fluoxetine may lead to the development of Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone secretion (SIADH), particularly in older adults.\",4\r\n8898,GASTROPARESIA,\"Gastroparesis is a medical condition that affects the stomach muscles, preventing proper stomach emptying. It is often observed in diabetic patients and can lead to significant weight loss, particularly in cases such as anorexia nervosa. The condition is characterized by delayed gastric emptying, which can result in increased gastric residue, especially in patients receiving enteral nutrition. Improvement in gastroparesis symptoms is typically achieved through nutritional rehabilitation and dietary adjustments.\",4\r\n4970,BULIMIA NERVIOSA,\"Bulimia Nervosa (BN) is an eating disorder characterized by recurrent episodes of binge eating, during which individuals consume large quantities of food in a short period. This behavior is typically followed by inappropriate compensatory actions aimed at preventing weight gain, such as self-induced vomiting, excessive exercise, or the misuse of laxatives. The disorder can lead to a range of serious health complications and often goes unnoticed by those around the affected individual. Treatment for Bulimia Nervosa generally requires a multifaceted approach that includes psychotherapy and medication to address both the psychological and physical aspects of the disorder.\",16\r\n2483,TRASTORNOS DE LA CONDUCTA ALIMENTARIA,\"\"\"TRASTORNOS DE LA CONDUCTA ALIMENTARIA\"\" refers to a range of psychological conditions that significantly impact eating behaviors and the relationship individuals have with food. These disorders, which include anorexia nerviosa (AN) among others, are characterized by abnormal or disturbed eating habits. They can complicate the management of obesity and necessitate specialized treatment approaches. Additionally, eating disorders can develop in children who are subjected to prolonged enteral nutrition, further affecting their relationship with food. Overall, \"\"TRASTORNOS DE LA CONDUCTA ALIMENTARIA\"\" encompasses various psychological disorders that require careful attention and intervention.\",10\r\n4966,DSM-5,\"The DSM-5, or the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Fifth Edition, is a comprehensive manual that provides standardized criteria for the classification and diagnosis of mental health conditions. It serves as an essential resource for mental health professionals, offering detailed criteria for diagnosing various disorders, including eating disorders such as anorexia nervosa and bulimia nerviosa. The DSM-5 is the updated version of the previous edition, DSM-IV, and includes revised criteria that reflect the latest research and understanding of mental health conditions. Overall, the DSM-5 plays a crucial role in the field of mental health by standardizing the diagnostic process and improving the accuracy of mental health assessments.\",4\r\n8878,TRASTORNO POR ATRACÓN,\"TRASTORNO POR ATRACÓN is an eating disorder that is characterized by recurrent episodes of binge eating, where individuals consume large quantities of food in a short period of time. Unlike other eating disorders, such as bulimia nervosa, individuals with trastorno por atracón do not engage in subsequent purging behaviors or inappropriate compensatory actions. This disorder often leads to significant distress for those affected. In summary, trastorno por atracón is defined by its compulsive eating episodes without the presence of purging, distinguishing it from other eating disorders.\",3\r\n4173,LEPTIN,\"Leptin is a hormone produced by adipose (fat) tissue that plays a crucial role in regulating energy balance and metabolism. It influences insulin sensitivity and appetite, particularly during pregnancy. Research has shown that leptin levels are significantly altered in patients with anorexia nervosa, where low levels of the hormone correlate with reduced body weight. This indicates that leptin is not only important for general energy regulation but also has specific implications for individuals with eating disorders, such as anorexia nervosa.\",3\r\n4445,T4,\"T4, also known as thyroxine, is a crucial thyroid hormone produced by the thyroid gland. It plays a significant role in regulating metabolism and is essential for normal growth and metabolic processes. T4 levels are often measured in thyroid function tests to assess thyroid health. In certain conditions, such as Anorexia Nervosa, T4 can be found at low-normal levels due to malnutrition, indicating a potential disruption in metabolic regulation. Additionally, T4 is converted into its active form, T3, in target cells, which is necessary for exerting its metabolic effects.\",3\r\n4444,T3,\"T3, or triiodothyronine, is a crucial thyroid hormone that plays a significant role in regulating metabolism and energy levels in the body. It is the more active form of thyroid hormone, derived from T4 (thyroxine) in target cells. T3 is particularly important for metabolic processes and is typically found at low levels in patients with Anorexia Nervosa, indicating its relevance in energy regulation and overall metabolic health.\",3\r\n8879,MUJERES ADOLESCENTES,\"Grupo demográfico que presenta una prevalencia significativa de trastornos de la conducta alimentaria, especialmente en países occidentales.\",2\r\n8752,TC,\"Tomografía Computarizada (TC) is a medical imaging technique utilized to visualize internal structures of the body. It plays a crucial role in diagnosing various medical conditions, including those related to anorexia and bulimia. However, the use of TC can be complicated in patients with morbid obesity due to their size, which may pose challenges in obtaining clear images.\",2\r\n8753,RM,\"La resonancia magnética (RM) es una técnica de imagen médica que proporciona imágenes detalladas de órganos y tejidos, siendo especialmente útil para evaluar cambios neurológicos en pacientes con trastornos alimentarios. Esta prueba diagnóstica puede ser difícil de realizar en pacientes con obesidad mórbida, lo que puede complicar su uso en esta población. La RM se utiliza para evaluar la salud de los tejidos blandos y del cerebro en pacientes que padecen trastornos alimentarios, lo que resalta su importancia en el diagnóstico y seguimiento de estas condiciones.\",2\r\n8911,EDUCATIONAL INSTITUTION,An institution that may provide psychological and nutritional education to patients recovering from eating disorders.,2\r\n8921,GHRELIN,\"Ghrelin is a hormone that plays a significant role in regulating appetite and influencing various hormonal releases, particularly in patients with anorexia nervosa. Research has shown that ghrelin levels are elevated in individuals suffering from anorexia nervosa, indicating a state of negative energy balance. This elevation suggests that ghrelin may be involved in the physiological responses associated with this eating disorder, highlighting its importance in understanding appetite regulation and energy homeostasis in affected patients.\",2\r\n3758,HYPOTHALAMUS,\"The hypothalamus is a critical region of the brain that plays a vital role in controlling the pituitary gland and regulating various hormonal functions within the body. It is integral to the endocrine system, influencing the secretion of adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) from the pituitary gland. Additionally, the hypothalamus is involved in the dysregulation that can lead to hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, particularly in patients suffering from Anorexia Nervosa. This highlights its importance not only in general hormone regulation but also in specific medical conditions related to hormonal imbalances.\",2\r\n8913,AMENORREA,\"Amenorrhea is defined as the absence of menstruation and can manifest in two forms: primary and secondary. This condition is often observed in individuals with Anorexia Nervosa, where it is primarily linked to hormonal imbalances. In patients suffering from Anorexia Nervosa, the disruption of normal hormonal function can lead to the cessation of menstrual cycles, highlighting the significant impact of this eating disorder on reproductive health.\",1\r\n4965,DSM-IV,\"The DSM-IV is a manual published by the American Psychiatric Association that outlines the criteria for diagnosing mental disorders, including anorexia nerviosa.\",1\r\n8880,MODELOS,Grupo profesional que parece estar más afectado por trastornos de la conducta alimentaria debido a presiones sociales sobre la imagen corporal.,1\r\n8882,GIMNASTAS,\"Grupo profesional que se encuentra en riesgo elevado de desarrollar trastornos de la conducta alimentaria, similar a modelos y atletas.\",1\r\n8886,FACTORES DE MAL PRONÓSTICO,\"Los factores de mal pronóstico en los trastornos de la conducta alimentaria incluyen vómitos, conductas purgativas y pérdida de peso importante.\",1\r\n8887,FACTORES DE BUEN PRONÓSTICO,Los factores de buen pronóstico incluyen el inicio en adolescentes jóvenes y una duración corta de la enfermedad.,1\r\n8922,ESTROGENS,Estrogens are hormones whose production decreases in patients with Anorexia Nerviosa due to loss of weight and fat mass.,1\r\n8923,MENSTRUATION,Menstruation recovery is an important indicator of healing in patients with Anorexia Nerviosa.,1\r\n8924,RT3,\"Reverse T3 (rT3) is a thyroid hormone that may be elevated in patients with Anorexia Nerviosa, indicating metabolic adaptation to malnutrition.\",1\r\n8881,ATLETAS,\"Grupo profesional que también muestra una mayor incidencia de trastornos de la conducta alimentaria, posiblemente debido a la presión para mantener un peso específico.\",1\r\n8888,ASOCIACIÓN AMERICANA DE PSIQUIATRÍA,\"The American Psychiatric Association is a professional organization that publishes diagnostic criteria for mental disorders, including eating disorders.\",1\r\n5535,5'-YODINASA,\"5'-iodinase is an enzyme that converts T4 to T3 in target cells, activating the thyroid hormone's effects.\",1\r\n5534,SRE,\"Steroid Response Elements (SRE) are specific DNA sequences that steroid hormone-receptor complexes bind to, regulating gene transcription.\",1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n3529,PACIENTE,ANOREXIA NERVIOSA,\"The patient is diagnosed with anorexia nerviosa, a condition that significantly impacts her ability to co